  "api_descriptions": "# emyDesign\n\n*class* `emyDesign`(*\\*args*, *\\*\\*kwargs*)\n\n1.  Block Domain: The emyDesign may contain an emyBlock , which holds all the design geometry, its physical connectivity and instances of other emyDesigns that lead down a design hierarchy. This is the block or physical domain.\n\n2.  Module Domain: The emyDesign may contain a number of emyModule objects that define a logical hierarchy. Several levels of this module hierarchy may live in a single emyDesign. This is the module or logical domain.\n\n3.  Occurrence Domain: In this domain every net and instance in the hierarchy has a unique representation, even when multiple instances of a module or a block cause the connectivity object to be reused.\n\nAll of these domains can be present in an emyDesign at the same time. The emyDesign also may contain parasitic data that provides an electrical model of the physical nets in the design.\n\n**emyDesigns in the Context of Pcell SuperMasters and SubMasters**\n\nemyDesigns also may be created as both SuperMasters and SubMasters of Pcells. To create a Pcell, you create an emyDesign and then you call `defineSuperMaster` on that emyDesign to turn it into a Pcell superMaster. The superMaster has a set of parameters with their default values.\n\nWhen creating an instance of Pcell superMaster, the call to create the instance can specify a parameter array that contains some or all of the parameters associated with the superMaster. The value in param array specified for an instance is an override parameter that specifies a non default value for this particular instance.\n\nUsually, the child node is created by creating an instance of Pcell and traversing the master node (i.e., Pcell child node) of the instance. The traversal process will be described below. You can also explicitly create a child master by calling `open` and specifying a set of parameter values. The sub host is not stored on disk, but only exists in memory.\n\nWhen an application traverses to the master for the Pcell instance, it will go to the subMaster emyDesign for that set of parameter values. The subMaster is created at run time by running the applications evaluation routine the first time the master is traversed to for a given parameter set for a given Pcell.\n\nThe caller must register a Pcell evaluation routine by constructing an emyPcellObserver that has an override for the virtual function onPreEval() or onPostEval(). When EmyDatabase needs to have a subMaster for a given parameterization of a superMaster, it calls the evaluator that has been registered for that superMaster.\n\n**Scratch Designs**\n\nA regular emyDesign lives in a cellView in an EmpyDatabase library and has a logical library, cell, and view for that `emyCellView` as well as having one or more physical database files on disk. A scratch emyDesign , on the other hand, lives only in memory and is associated with a library only for its technology information. A scratch emyDesign will not require a physical file on disk.\n\nEmpyDatabase uses a form of scratch emyDesigns for Pcell subMasters and emyStdVia masters. A Pcell subMaster exists only in memory although its library, cell, and view specification match that of its corresponding superMaster. An emyStdVia master also only exists in memory and has no library, cell, or view per se other than what EmpyDatabase gives it. These types of designs can be differentiated from other designs by querying with the `isSubMaster` and `isStdViaMaster` functions. These types of designs will also have the mode ‘s’.\n\nYou can open your own scratch emyDesign using one of the `openScratch` functions. Because no database file is required, no cell or view is required either. A library is required to scope the design to the library’s technology data and a viewType is required to designate the design’s intended contents. A scratch design can be used as a scratch-pad area since it cannot be saved directly to disk. A scratch design opened with `openScratch` can be used like a regular design, except for the following restrictions:\n\n- It cannot be directly saved in any manner.\n\n- It cannot be used as a custom via master.\n\n- It can be used as an instance master only within another explicit scratch design.\n\nA scratch design can be saved to disk using `saveAs` , but some restrictions apply. Scratch designs you open will have the mode ‘S’.\n\nA scratch design will have library, cell, and view names associated with it, but scratch designs are managed in a separate name scope from regular designs. Thus, it is possible to create a regular design and a scratch design using the same library, cell, and view names. The version of `openScratch` that automatically names the design generates a cell name prefixed with the string ‘[S\\_\\_](#id2)’ and uses a viewName derived from the specified viewType.\n\n`areTimeStampsEnabled`()\n**`areTimeStampsEnabled() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if the timeStamp modification is enabled on this design.\n\n`calcVMSize`()\n**`calcVMSize() -\\> emyUInt8`**\n\nCalculate and return the amount of virtual memory this design is currently using.\n\n`close`()\n**`close()`**\n\nDeprecated since version v2023.12: This function will be removed in a future version. We strongly advise using `aeCloseDesign` or `dbCloseCV` in new code.\n\nEnd the operation of the current program on the specified design. After calling this function, other programs can operate this design.\n\nEach emyDesign has a reference count, which is the sum of the calls to `open` on it, plus the number of other open emyDesigners with InstHeaders referencing it, minus the number of calls to `close`. Each successful call to close will reduce the reference count on the design.\n\nWhen closing causes the reference count to decrease to 0, design will be cleared or deleted from memory. Pointers to it and to all managed objects included in the design become invalid. If this happens when the design is never saved, its cellView, cell and view will be deleted, otherwise they will be empty. Use `purge` to force this operation, even for non-zero reference counts.\n\nApplications are not required to close designs. If a design is not closed. it remains in memory until the process ends. Applications that want to reclaim memory used by open designs may use `close` calls to indicate that they are done with the design. In this use model, calls to `close` will match calls to `open`. There should never be more calls to :func:’close’ for a given design than calls to `open`.\n\nImportant\n\nIn `emyDesign`, `open` and `close` must be used together.\n\nRaises\nemcPurgeOnDesignBeingPurged :\n\n`contains`(*design*)\n**`contains(design: emyDesign) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nDetermine whether the current design references the specified design.\n\n`defineSuperMaster`(*\\_def*, *pa*)\n**`defineSuperMaster(def: emyPcellDef, pa: emyParamArray)`**\n\nCreates or redefines the super supervisor design. The existing data in this design remains complete, and the Pcell parameters and the new Pcell evaluation interface information are bound to the design. Super administrators often use default parameter values to include the implementation of Pcell.\n\nIf this superMaster design already has existing subMasters, then those subMasters will be reevaluated. If this call has a different set of parameters than previously existed, or if this design was not previously a supermaster, then all instances bound to this supermaster will be realigned with new submasters as follows:\n\n- If the instance has any parameters that no longer exist on the superMaster, those parameters are converted to properties on the instance\n\n- If new parameters are added to this superMaster, then the properties on the instance will checked for a property of the same name which will then be converted to a parameter\n\n- Each instance will be assigned to a new subInstHeader reflecting its new parameterization\n\n- The new subInstHeaders will be bound to an appropriate new subMaster\n\nNote\n\nThis process may result in a different grouping of instances to subMasters and a different number of subMasters.\n\nRaises\nemcCannotModifyReadModeDesign :\n\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\nemcPcellObserverBindFailed :\n\nemcPcellInvalidPcellDef :\n\nemcCannotDefineSuperMasterWithParasitics :\n\n*static* `destroy`(*\\*args*)\n**`destroy(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName)`**\n\n**`destroy(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, type: emySaveRecoverType)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`destroy(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName)`**\n\n  > Removes the current design when it exists and is not in the open state.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcDestroyCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcDestroyDesignOpen :\n\n- **`destroy(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, type: emySaveRecoverType)`**\n\n  > Remove the specified recover file from disk. It throws an exception if the file is not found.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libName :\n  > The library in which the cell is located.\n  >\n  > cellName :\n  > The name of the design for the specified recover file.\n  >\n  > viewName :\n  > The name of the cellView for the design.\n  >\n  > type :\n  > The type of recover file to remove.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcLibNotFound :\n  >\n  > emcDestroyCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcSaveRecoverFileDoesNotExistOnDisk :\n\n`enableTimeStamps`(*value=True*)\n**`enableTimeStamps(value: emyBoolean = True)`**\n\nEnable or disable timeStamp modification on this design. By default, timeStamp modification is enabled.\n\n`enableUndo`(*\\*args*)\n**`enableUndo(value: emyBoolean = True)`**\n\n**`enableUndo(value: emyBoolean, undoModel: emyUndoModelEnum)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`enableUndo(value: emyBoolean = True)`**\n\nEnable or disable undo for this design. If enabled, all changes made to this design will be recorded so that undo () can restore the design to an earlier state. If disabled, all *undo* information will be discarded and changes will not be recorded.\n\nThis function is deprecated. It is recommended that applications instead use the enableUndo function that allows specification of a model for undo operations.\n\n- **`enableUndo(value: emyBoolean, undoModel: emyUndoModelEnum)`**\n\nThis feature enables or disables undo for this design. If enabled, all changes made to this design will be recorded so that undo () can restore the design to an earlier state. If disabled, all *undo* information will be discarded and changes will not be recorded. This function requires the caller to specify a specific model (undomodel) for undo.\n\nEmpyDatabase supports two undo models:\n\n- postCheckpoint model, in which checkpoints are set after an action.\n\n- preCheckpoint model, in which checkpoints are set before an action.\n\nIf postCheckpoint is specified, a call to undo must immediately follow a checkpoint or an exception is thrown.\n\n**Raises:**\n\nemcDesignInvalidUndoModel :\n\n`evalSuperMaster`()\n**`evalSuperMaster()`**\n\nPopulates the Supermaster with default parameter values, which contains the contents of this Pcell variable built using these values. The generated content is added to any previous content of the design.\n\nThe main purpose is to create some meaningful content for super administrators and view it in the graphic viewer. This evaluation has no impact on or relationship with the pcell instance.\n\nRaises\nemcCannotModifyReadModeDesign :\n\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\nemcPcellInvalidPcellDef :\n\n*static* `exists`(*\\*args*)\n**`exists(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\n**`exists(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, type: emySaveRecoverType) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`exists(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\n  > Returns true if the current design of the specified lib, cell, view names is open, or if the design is found on disk and is not empty.\n  >\n  > Note\n  >\n  > This function is not limited to the viewTypes valid for design databases. ven if this function returns true, `open` will throw an exception if the viewType of the cellView is not a valid design viewType. See `open` for more information.\n\n- **`exists(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, type: emySaveRecoverType) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\n  > Returns true if the specified emySaveRecoverType file is found on disk.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The name of the library where the cell is located.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The name of the design for which the recover type is checked.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The name of the cellView.\n  >\n  > type :\n  > The recover type of either emcAutoSaveType or emcCriticalSaveType.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcDestroyCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcInterfaceException :\n\n*static* `find`(*libNameIn*, *cellNameIn*, *viewNameIn*)\n**`find(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nReturns design when the design of the specified lib, cell, view names is found and it is open, otherwise None is returned. Calling find() does not increase the reference count for design.\n\n`findParam`(*name*, *param*)\n**`findParam(name: emyString, param: emyParam) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nSearches the parameter list of this design to find the parameter with the specified *name*. If found, it will fill in *param* with the appropriate attributes and return true. Otherwise, the function returns false.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidPcellMaster :\n\n*static* `findScratch`(*libNameIn*, *cellNameIn*, *viewNameIn*)\n**`findScratch(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nReturns design when the scratch design of the specified lib, cell, view names is found and it is open, otherwise None is returned.\n\n`getAnalysisPoints`()\n**`getAnalysisPoints() -\\> emyCollection_emyAnalysisPoint_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of analysisPoints in this design.\n\n`getAppDefsByDataType`(*dataType*, *domain*)\n**`getAppDefsByDataType(dataType: emyDesignDataType, domain: emyDomain) -\\> emyCollection_emyAppDef_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of emyAppDefs in this design for the specified dataType and domain.\n\nParameters\n**dataType :**\nThe data type on which to search for.\n\n**domain :**\nThe domain on which to search for appDefs.\n\n`getAppObjectDefs`()\n**`getAppObjectDefs() -\\> emyCollection_emyAppObjectDef_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of object extension types used in this design. Object extensions are independent objects that are not bound to built-in design objects. See `emyAppObjectDef` for the usage of object extensions.\n\n`getAppObjects`(*\\_def*)\n**`getAppObjects(def: emyAppObjectDef) -\\> emyCollection_emyAppObject_emyDesign`**\n\nGets a collection of emyAppObjects in this design for the specified extension.\n\n`getCellName`(*\\*args*)\n**`getCellName(cellName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n**`getCellName(ns: emyNameSpace, cellName: emyString)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getCellName(cellName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n  > Returns the name of the cell related to the current design form of an emyScalarName.\n\n- **`getCellName(ns: emyNameSpace, cellName: emyString)`**\n\n  > Returns the cellName using the specified nameSpace.\n\n`getCellType`()\n**`getCellType() -\\> emyCellType`**\n\nReturns the cellType for this design.\n\n`getConstraintGroupHeaders`()\n**`getConstraintGroupHeaders() -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintGroupHeader_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of constraint group headers in this database.\n\n`getConstraintGroups`(*\\*args*)\n**`getConstraintGroups() -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintGroup_emyDesign`**\n\n**`getConstraintGroups(def: emyConstraintGroupDef) -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintGroup_emyDesign`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getConstraintGroups() -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintGroup_emyDesign`**\n\n  > Returns a collection of constraint groups in this database.\n\n- **`getConstraintGroups(def: emyConstraintGroupDef) -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintGroup_emyDesign`**\n\n  > Returns the collection of constraint groups associated with the specified constraintGroupDef in this design database.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > def :\n  > Pointer to an emyConstraintGroupDef.\n\n`getConstraintParams`()\n**`getConstraintParams() -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraintParam_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of constraint parameters in this database.\n\n`getConstraints`(*\\_def=None*)\n**`getConstraints(def: emyConstraintDef = None) -\\> emyCollection_emyConstraint_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns the collection of constraints with the specified definition in this design database. If no definition is specified, all constraints are returned.\n\nParameters\n**def :**\nOptional Constraint definition to search for.\n\n`getCpID`()\n**`getCpID() -\\> emyUInt4`**\n\nReturns the ID of the last checkpoint set in the current design. If the ID is not set on the checkpoint, this function returns UINT_MAX.\n\n`getCreateTime`()\n**`getCreateTime() -\\> emyTime`**\n\nReturn the creation time of the current design.\n\n`getDataCompressionLevel`()\n**`getDataCompressionLevel() -\\> emyInt4`**\n\nReturns an integer indicating the compression level of Database disk data. If the level is zero, an exception will be thrown (it may be that the data on the disk is not compressed, or the design has not been saved to the disk, or the design is opened in temporary mode).\n\nRaises\nemcDesignNotCompressed :\n\n`getDefaultConstraintGroup`()\n**`getDefaultConstraintGroup() -\\> emyConstraintGroup`**\n\nIf there is a default constraint group in the current design, the existing one will be returned. If it does not exist, a new constraint group will be created and returned.\n\n`getEffectiveUndoModel`()\n**`getEffectiveUndoModel() -\\> emyUndoModelEnum`**\n\nReturn the undo model if the application explicitly sets the undo model. If the undo model is not explicitly set, this function will return the undo model ‘valid’ according to whether the last record on the undo stack is a checkpoint. If the last record is a checkpoint, the model is assumed to be emcUndoModelPostCheckpoint. If the last record is not a checkpoint, the model is effectively assumed to be emcUndoModelPreCheckpoint.\n\n`getFeatures`(*features*)\n**`getFeatures(dataModelRev: emyUInt4, features: emyFeatureArray)`**\n\nReturns the data model revision number for the database, as well as an array of the features currently in the database. The application can iterate through the array to get the ID for each feature present. Each feature ID corresponds to an emyFeatureIDEnum value.\n\nParameters\n**dataModelRev :**\nreturned data model revision.\n\n**features :**\nreturned feature array.\n\n`getGroups`(*\\*args*)\n**`getGroupsByName(name: emyString, filterFlags: emyUInt4 = emcGroupIterNoDomain) -\\> emyCollection_emyGroup_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of groups with the specified name in this design. The filter flag can be used to restrict the search to groups in specific domains other than modules, blocks, and appearance domains, or groups without domain restrictions on their members.\n\nThe default value of filter flags limits the collection to groups without domain specific membership. If more than one domain selection flag is enabled in the incoming filter flag, an exception is thrown. flags.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidGroupByNameIterFlags :\n\n`getGroupsByName`(*name*, *filterFlags=1*)\n\n`getLastSavedTime`()\n**`getLastSavedTime() -\\> emyTime`**\n\nReturns the last time the current design was saved.\n\nReturns\nemyTime The time since 00:00:00 GMT, Jan. 1, 1970, measured in seconds.\n\n`getLib`()\n**`getLib() -\\> emyLib`**\n\nReturns the Lib related to the current design, and the return value is the object of emyLib.\n\nRaises\nemcCannotFindObjectForViaMaster :\n\n`getLibName`(*\\*args*)\n**`getLibName(libName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n**`getLibName(ns: emyNameSpace, libName: emyString)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getLibName(libName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n  > Returns the name of the library related to the current design form of an emyScalarName.\n\n- **`getLibName(ns: emyNameSpace, libName: emyString)`**\n\n  > Returns the library name using the specified nameSpace.\n\n`getMode`()\n**`getMode() -\\> emyChar`**\n\nReturns the mode of the currently open design.\n\nWhen the design is a scratch design or a submaster of pCell, returns ‘s’ or’S’ which correspond to modes used internally by EmpyDatabase.\n\n`getNumUndoCpRecs`()\n**`getNumUndoCpRecs() -\\> emyUInt4`**\n\nRecords the number of undo records on the latest checkpoint saved by the current design.\n\n`getNumUndoRecsOnCp`()\n**`getNumUndoRecsOnCp() -\\> emyUInt4`**\n\nReturns the number of undo records that will be removed if an undo command is issued. The value returned depends on the undo model in use. If the undo model is emcUndoModelPostCheckpoint, the function returns the number of undo records before the last checkpoint. If the undo model is emcUndoModelPreCheckpoint, the function returns the number of undo records after the last checkpoint.\n\n*static* `getOpenDesigns`(*\\*args*)\n**`getOpenDesigns() -\\> emyCollection_emyDesign_emyDesign`**\n\n**`getOpenDesigns(filterFlags: emyUInt4) -\\> emyCollection_emyDesign_emyDesign`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getOpenDesigns() -\\> emyCollection_emyDesign_emyDesign`**\n\n  > Returns a list of open designs that are not temporary designs.\n\n- **`getOpenDesigns(filterFlags: emyUInt4) -\\> emyCollection_emyDesign_emyDesign`**\n\n  > Returns a collection of open designs filtered by the given filter flags. The filter flag bits may be logically “OR’d together to refine the contents of the collection.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > filterFlags :\n  > Specifies what the collection contains. The bit flags are defined as follows:\n  >\n  > - *emcDesignIterAll:* the collection contains all open designs.\n  >\n  > - *emcDesignIterRegular:* the collection contains open designs that are not Pcell subMasters, not stdVia masters and not scratch designs.\n  >\n  > - *emcDesignIterPCellSubMaster:* the collection contains all open Pcell subMasters.\n  >\n  > - *emcDesignIterStdViaMaster:* the collection contains all open stdVia masters.\n  >\n  > - *emcDesignIterScratch:* the collection contains all open scratch designs.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDesignIterFlags :\n\n`getParams`(*\\*args*)\n**`getParams(params: emyParamArray)`**\n\n**`getParams(params: emyParamArray, includeDefaults: emyBoolean)`**\n\n**`getParams(params: emyViaParam)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getParams(params: emyParamArray)`**\n\n  > Fills out *pArray* with the parameters attached to this design. The *pArray* will include all parameters whether or not they were explicitly specified during creation of a subMaster (either from an `open` or Pcell evaluation). An exception will be thrown if the design is not a superMaster or a subMaster.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidPcellMaster :\n\n- **`getParams(params: emyParamArray, includeDefaults: emyBoolean)`**\n\n  > Fills out *pArray* with the parameters attached to this design. If *includeDefaults* is true, *pArray* will contain all parameters whether or not they were explicitly specified during creation of a subMaster (either from an `open` or Pcell evaluation). If *includeDefaults* is false, only parameters that were specified during creation of the subMaster will returned in *pArray*. On a superMaster, an empty array will be returned in *pArray* if *includeDefaults* is false. An exception will be thrown if the design is not a superMaster or a subMaster.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidPcellMaster :\n\n- **`getParams(params: emyViaParam)`**\n\n  > Fills out the *vParams* with the viaParams attached to this design.\n  >\n  > If the emyDesign object is a standard via master, the\\*vParams\\*contains the parameters associated with the emyStdVia object used to create the via’s master design.\n  >\n  > An exception is thrown if this design is not an emyStdVia master.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidStdViaMaster :\n\n`getPcellDef`()\n**`getPcellDef() -\\> emyPcellDef`**\n\nReturns a pointer to an `emyPcellDef` in this superMaster design.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\n`getPcellEvaluatorName`(*evalName*)\n**`getPcellEvaluatorName(evalName: emyString)`**\n\nReturns the name of the Pcell evaluator associated with this superMaster design.\n\nParameters\n**evalName :**\nThe returned name of the Pcell evaluator associated with this superMaster design.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\n`getReadInTime`()\n**`getReadInTime() -\\> emyTime`**\n\nReturns the time when the current design was read from the disk.\n\nReturns\nemyTime The time since 00:00:00 GMT, Jan. 1, 1970, measured in seconds.\n\n`getRefCount`()\n**`getRefCount() -\\> emyUInt4`**\n\nReturns the number of times this design was opened without being closed. For each `open` that references this emyDesign, this number will be incremented. For each `close` called on it, the number is decremented. For each open emyDesign (excluding this design) that has an InstHeader that references this design, it will also be increased. For more information, see `close`.\n\nReturns\nemyUInt4\n\n*static* `getRevNumber`()\n**`getRevNumber() -\\> emyUInt4`**\n\nReturns the revision number of the internal format for the design format.\n\nReturns\nemyUInt4\n\n`getSubMasters`()\n**`getSubMasters() -\\> emyCollection_emyDesign_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of subMasters in this design. An emcInvalidSuperMaster exception is thrown if the design is not a superMaster.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\n`getSuperMaster`()\n**`getSuperMaster() -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a emyDesign pointer of pCell superMaster.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidSubMaster :\n\n`getTech`()\n**`getTech() -\\> emyTech`**\n\nReturn the technology database associated with the current design.\n\n`getTimeStamp`(*\\*args*)\n**`getTimeStamp(type: emyDesignDataType) -\\> emyTimeStamp`**\n\n**`getTimeStamp(def: emyAppObjectDef) -\\> emyTimeStamp`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getTimeStamp(type: emyDesignDataType) -\\> emyTimeStamp`**\n\n  > Returns the timeStamp value for the objects of the specified data type in this design.\n  >\n  > If no data object of this design is given, the default timestamp will be returned.\n  >\n  > Timestamps are not available for the following types of design objects and it is an error if the given data type is one of these values.\n  >\n  > - emcDesignParamDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccNetDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccBusNetDefDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccTermDataType\n  >\n  > - emcBusTermDefDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccInstHeaderDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccModuleInstHeaderDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccInstDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccVectorInstDefDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccInstTermDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccConnectDefDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccAssignmentDataType\n  >\n  > - emcOccOccurrenceDataType\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDataTypeForTimeStamp :\n\n- **`getTimeStamp(def: emyAppObjectDef) -\\> emyTimeStamp`**\n\n  > Returns the timestamp value of the application object in the specified application object definition design. If there is no such object in the database, the default value timestamp will be returned.\n\n`getTopBlock`()\n**`getTopBlock() -\\> emyBlock`**\n\nIf the design has a block, return the object of emyBlock; otherwise, return None.\n\nNote\n\nThe name of this call reflects the possibility of embedding the block hierarchy in a single design in the future. At this time, there can only be one block in the design.\n\n`getUndoModel`()\n**`getUndoModel() -\\> emyUndoModelEnum`**\n\nReturns the undo model specified when undo is enabled.\n\n`getUsedIn`()\n**`getUsedIn() -\\> emyCollection_emyInstHeader_emyDesign`**\n\nIndicate which design the current design is referenced by. And returns the collection of instHeaders of referenced designs. It does not open any designs or return any information from designs that are only on disk. This function can be used to traverse “up” a design hierarchy that has been read into memory.\n\n`getValues`()\n**`getValues() -\\> emyCollection_emyValue_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns a collection of values in this database.\n\n`getViaHeaderUsedIn`()\n**`getViaHeaderUsedIn() -\\> emyCollection_emyViaHeader_emyDesign`**\n\nIndicate which design the current design is referenced by. And returns the collection of viaHeaders of referenced designs. It does not open any designs or return any information from designs that are only on disk. This function can be used to traverse “up” a design hierarchy that has been read into memory.\n\n`getViaVariantHeaders`()\n**`getViaVariantHeaders() -\\> emyCollection_emyViaVariantHeader_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns the collection of emyViaVariantHeaders associated with this design.\n\n`getViaVariants`()\n**`getViaVariants() -\\> emyCollection_emyViaVariant_emyDesign`**\n\nReturns the collection of emyViaVariants associated with this design.\n\n`getViewName`(*\\*args*)\n**`getViewName(viewName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n**`getViewName(ns: emyNameSpace, viewName: emyString)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`getViewName(viewName: emyScalarName)`**\n\n  > Returns the name of the view related to the current design form of an emyScalarName.\n\n- **`getViewName(ns: emyNameSpace, viewName: emyString)`**\n\n  > Returns the name of the view associated with this design using the specified nameSpace.\n\n`getViewType`()\n**`getViewType() -\\> emyViewType`**\n\nReturns the viewtype represented by this design. If the design is created as a follower in cellview, the view type will be different from that of the cellview master file.\n\n`hasDefaultConstraintGroup`()\n**`hasDefaultConstraintGroup() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if there is a default constraint group for this design.\n\n`hasRecursion`()\n**`hasRecursion() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a Boolean value indicating whether there is a recursive loop in the design hierarchy starting from the design.\n\nReturns\nTrue If the design contains a recursive loop in its hierarchy.\n\n`hasReference`(*design*)\n**`hasReference(design: emyDesign) -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a Boolean value indicating whether the design hierarchy starting from this design has a recursive reference to the specified main structure.\n\nParameters\n**master :**\nLooks for a reference to this master design in the hierarchy.\n\nReturns\nTrue If a reference to the master is found.\n\n`hasSymmetricConnectivity`()\n**`hasSymmetricConnectivity() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if the design contains only one module (that is the top module) and one block. If it returns true, it means that the design connectivity in the block domain and the module domain is the same, except for block objects and hidden block objects.\n\n`isConsistent`()\n**`isConsistent() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\n`isDataCompressed`()\n**`isDataCompressed() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if the on-disk data of the database is compressed.\n\n`isInUndo`()\n**`isInUndo() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if the current design is in undo or redo operation.\n\n`isModified`()\n**`isModified() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nIf the current design is modified but not saved, return True. Otherwise returns False.\n\n`isPurging`()\n**`isPurging() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true when the program is clearing design. Design is valid when onPurge notification is sent. Application watchers, which are universal in nature, can use this API instead of clearing the scope notification to know when to clear the design.\n\n`isRecovered`()\n**`isRecovered() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns True if this design was recovered from a critical save or an auto save database file.\n\n`isScalarized`()\n**`isScalarized() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a Boolean value indicating whether all explicit multi bit connectivity in any domain of this design is scalarized. Explicit multi bit connectivity is represented by the bus network, bundlenet, bunderms, bundlerms, and vectorinst explicitly created by the application.\n\nExplicit multi bit connectivity should be scalarized to ensure that any corresponding bitNets, bitTerms, and bitInsts are promoted to explicit States and can be used to support implementation data. The corresponding multi bit connectivity is degraded to an implicit state, so that implementations are no longer associated with them, and restrictions will lead to explicit modifications inconsistent with the unit connectivity.\n\n`isScratch`()\n**`isScratch() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nreturns a boolean whether this design represents a scratch design.\n\n`isStdViaMaster`()\n**`isStdViaMaster() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a boolean whether this design represents a stdVia master.\n\n`isSubMaster`()\n**`isSubMaster() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a boolean whether this design represents a pCell subMaster.\n\n`isSuperMaster`()\n**`isSuperMaster() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a boolean whether this design represents a pCell superMaster.\n\n`isTechBound`()\n**`isTechBound() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nDetermines if this design is bound to its technology database.\n\nReturns true if this design is bound to its technology database. Otherwise returns False.\n\n`isUndoEnabled`()\n**`isUndoEnabled() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns true if it is determined that undo is enabled for the current design.\n\n`isWriting`()\n**`isWriting() -\\> emyBoolean`**\n\nReturns a Boolean value reflecting whether the specified design is being written to the disk. The writing process starts after sending the pre save and pre save notifications and ends before sending the post save and post save notifications. This function is very useful in error handling code, such as a signal handler, which traverses open databases and wants to check whether any databases are being written when an error occurs.\n\n`minimizeVM`()\n**`minimizeVM()`**\n\nMinimize the amount of virtual memory currently used by this design. It releases dynamically assigned data structures and can be rebuilt as needed.\n\nThe application can call this function after creating an emyDesign or making important edits to an existing emyDesign, and then start the processing phase of interacting with the design in different ways.\n\nBe called with caution because it will have a significant impact on the performance of subsequent operations.\n\n*static* `open`(*\\*args*)\nDeprecated since version v2023.12: This function will be removed in a future version. We strongly advise using `aeOpenDesign` or `dbOpenCV` in new code.\n\n**`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType, mode: emyChar) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n**`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, mode: emyChar) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n**`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, params: emyParamArray) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n**`open(viaDef: emyStdViaDef, params: emyViaParam = None) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n**`open(viaDef: emyCustomViaDef, params: emyParamArray = None) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType, mode: emyChar) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Open the design with the specified library, cell, and view name with the specified access mode. If successful, return the design. This version opens the design using the viewType attribute of the emyViewType object pointed to by *viewType*.\n  >\n  > Write a new design because it can specify a viewType for the design. When used to read or modify a design, it will succeed only if the design was previously created with the specified viewType. Normally, the design to be opened must be the main file in cellView. However, if cellView has a non-design view type, this function can be used to open the non-primary database.\n  >\n  > the library container is temporarily locked when users open a library for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend with this function. If another user attempts to use `open` to ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend to the library, an emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess exception is thrown. This behavior protects against another process opening the same library for write or append access. Note that having a design open for write access does not interfere with another process reading the database.\n  >\n  > When you open a database in ‘w’rite mode and then save the database, the mode changes to ‘a’ppend. When you open a database in ‘A’ppend mode and then save that database, the mode changes to ‘a’ppend.\n  >\n  > After opening the library for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend, EmyDatabase creates a database file and a lock file for you. After this point, the access to the library container is once again opened for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend mode to other users. However, anyone attempting to access the locked database file will get an emcCannotLockDatabase exception.\n  >\n  > Each open() increments the reference count on this design (see `close` for further discussion).\n  >\n  > Note\n  >\n  > Although users can construct their own emyViewTypes, when opening a design with this function, valid viewTypes are restricted to a subset of emyReservedViewTypes, namely, an `emyReservedViewType` object constructed with one of the following enumerated constants: *emcMaskLayout*, *emcSchematic*, *emcSchematicSymbol*, *emcNetlist*, or *emcHierDesign*. An exception is thrown if viewType is not one of these reserved design viewTypes.\n  >\n  > Standard practice is to call `emyViewType.get` to construct an emyViewType object using one of the valid reserved design viewType enumerated constants, then pass a pointer to the constructed emyViewType object to `open` via the viewType parameter.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The existing library name.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The cell name.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The view name.\n  >\n  > viewType :\n  > A pointer to the viewType.\n  >\n  > mode :\n  > The mode in which to open the design:\n  >\n  > - ‘r’ for read. - ‘w’ for write.\n  >\n  > - ‘a’ for append.\n  >\n  > - ‘A’ for append in override mode, which is not typically used.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  > If the lib name doesn’t exist, or in read mode if the cell or view doesn’t exist.\n  >\n  > emcOpenOnDesignBeingPurged :\n  >\n  > emcOpenUndefinedModeDesign :\n  >\n  > emcMismatchViewTypeForOpen :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpen :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmpyDatabase and cannot be opened.\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpenInAppendMode :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmpyDatabase and cannot be opened in append mode.\n  >\n  > emcDesignPrimaryFileDoesNotExist :\n  >\n  > emcDesignFollowerFileDoesNotExist :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDatabaseFile :\n  >\n  > emcFileNotWritable :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDesignViewType :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetReadAccess :\n  >\n  > emcLibAccessCannotUpgrade :\n  >\n  > emcDesignHasReadNeedWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcCannotRepairCorruptedAppData :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSteinerConnRoutes :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedMustJoinTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSupplySensitivityTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedGroundSensitivityTerms :\n\n- **`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, mode: emyChar) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Open the design with the specified library, cell, and view name with the specified access mode. If successful, return the design. This version opens the design using the viewType attribute of the emyViewType object pointed to by viewType.\n  >\n  > This parameter transfer method can be used to open an existing design for reading or modifying. If this open function is called to create (write) a new design, an exception will be thrown because each design needs the specification of viewType when it is created.\n  >\n  > When you open a library for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend with this function, the library container is temporarily locked. If another user attempts to use `open` to ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend to the library, an emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess exception is thrown. This behavior protects against\\*another\\*process opening the same library for write or append access.\n  >\n  > When you open a database in ‘w’rite mode and then save the database, the mode changes to ‘a’ppend. When you open a database in ‘A’ppend mode and then save that database, the mode changes to ‘a’ppend.\n  >\n  > After opening the library for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend, EmpyDatabase creates a database file and a lock file for you. After this point, the access to the library container is once again opened for ‘w’rite, ‘a’ppend, or ‘A’ppend mode to other users. However, anyone attempting to access the locked database file will get an emcCannotLockDatabase exception.\n  >\n  > Each open() increments the reference count on this design (see `close` for further discussion).\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The existing library name.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The existing cell name of the existing library.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The existing view name of the existing library.\n  >\n  > mode :\n  > The mode in which to open the existing design:\n  >\n  > - ‘r’ for read. - ‘w’ for write.\n  >\n  > - ‘a’ for append.\n  >\n  > - ‘A’ for append in override mode, which is not typically used.\n  >\n  > Note\n  >\n  > The design must already exist, even when opening for write.\n  >\n  > **Returns:**\n  >\n  > > An open design object or throws an exception.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  > If the lib name doesn’t exist, or in read mode if the cell or view doesn’t exist\n  >\n  > emcOpenOnDesignBeingPurged :\n  >\n  > emcOpenUndefinedModeDesign :\n  > The mode character is illegal\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDatabaseFile :\n  >\n  > emcFileNotWritable :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpen :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmyDatabase and cannot be opened.\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpenInAppendMode :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmyDatabase and cannot be opened in append mode.\n  >\n  > emcDesignPrimaryFileDoesNotExist :\n  >\n  > emcDesignFollowerFileDoesNotExist :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetReadAccess :\n  >\n  > emcLibAccessCannotUpgrade :\n  >\n  > emcDesignHasReadNeedWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcCannotRepairCorruptedAppData :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSteinerConnRoutes :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedMustJoinTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSupplySensitivityTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedGroundSensitivityTerms :\n\n- **`open(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, params: emyParamArray) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Find or create a submaster for the given supermaster emyDesign. If the submaster already exists, it is returned and its reference count (see `close` for further discussion of reference counts) is incremented.\n  >\n  > If the supermaster is in memory but does not have an appropriate submaster, a submaster will be created. If SuperMaster is not already open, it will search for emyDesign of any viewType locally in the given library and open it in read mode. It will then create the appropriate submaster.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindDesign :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidSuperMaster :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDatabaseFile :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcCannotRepairCorruptedAppData :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSteinerConnRoutes :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedMustJoinTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedSupplySensitivityTerms :\n  >\n  > emcCorruptedGroundSensitivityTerms :\n\n- **`open(viaDef: emyStdViaDef, params: emyViaParam = None) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Return the viaMaster associated with the specified emyStdViaDef object. If a viaMaster does not exist, one is created and it is returned. If the VIA argument array is not specified, the default argument associated with a given *viaDef* is used.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > viaDef :\n  > The standard viaDef.\n  >\n  > params :\n  > The via parameter array.\n\n- **`open(viaDef: emyCustomViaDef, params: emyParamArray = None) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Open design associated with the specified emyCustomViaDef. If an array of arguments is specified, the submaster is returned. An exception is thrown in two cases: if an array of parameters is specified and design is not a parameterized cell; Second, design does not have parameters.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > viaDef :\n  > The custom viaDef.\n  >\n  > params :\n  > The parameter array.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindDesign :\n\n`openHier`(*\\*args*)\n**`openHier()`**\n\n**`openHier(numLevels: emyUInt4)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`openHier()`**\n\n  > Uses the current design as the root to try to recursively open the hierarchy of the whole design.\n\n- **`openHier(numLevels: emyUInt4)`**\n\n  > Open the design hierarchy numLevels deep, with this design as the root.\n\n*static* `openScratch`(*\\*args*)\n**`openScratch(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n**`openScratch(libNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`openScratch(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Scratch design with the specified lib/cell/view names. If successful, the design is returned.\n  >\n  > Since scratch design cannot be saved directly to disk, you can use scratch design as a scratch-pad area. You can use `saveAs` to save scratch design to disk, but there are some limitations. The scratch design that is opened will have the mode ‘S’ internally.\n  >\n  > A scratch design can be used like a regular design, except for the following restrictions:\n  >\n  > - It cannot be directly saved in any manner.\n  >\n  > - It cannot be used as a custom via master.\n  >\n  > - It can be used as an instance master only within another explicit scratch design.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The existing library name.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The cell name.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The view name.\n  >\n  > viewType :\n  > The viewType object.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotPerformOperationInMultipleThreadsUseModel :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDesignViewType :\n  >\n  > emcMismatchViewTypeForOpen :\n  >\n  > emcOpenOnDesignBeingPurged :\n\n- **`openScratch(libNameIn: emyScalarName, viewType: emyViewType) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\n  > Open temporary design with the specified library name. If successful, design is returned. CellName will be automatically generated by EMY. ViewName will be derived from the specified viewType.\n  >\n  > Since scratch design cannot be saved directly to disk, you can use scratch design as a scratch-pad area. You can use `saveAs` to save scratch design to disk, but there are some limitations. The scratch design that is opened will have the mode ‘S’ internally.\n  >\n  > A scratch design can be used like a regular design, except for the following restrictions:\n  >\n  > - It cannot be directly saved in any manner.\n  >\n  > - It cannot be used as a custom via master.\n  >\n  > - It can be used as an instance master only within another explicit scratch design.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The name of an existing library.\n  >\n  > viewType :\n  > The viewType object.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotPerformOperationInMultipleThreadsUseModel :\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcInvalidDesignViewType :\n  >\n  > emcMismatchViewTypeForOpen :\n  >\n  > emcOpenOnDesignBeingPurged :\n\n`preAllocate`(*\\*args*)\n**`preAllocate(dataType: emyDesignDataTypeEnum, numObjs: emyUInt4)`**\n\n**`preAllocate(type: emyType, numObjs: emyUInt4)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`preAllocate(dataType: emyDesignDataTypeEnum, numObjs: emyUInt4)`**\n\n  > Pre allocates a specified number of objects of the specified *dataType*. This is useful for applications that know in advance how many specific types of objects need to be created.\n\n- **`preAllocate(type: emyType, numObjs: emyUInt4)`**\n\n  > Pre allocate the number of emyType objects specified by the ‘numObjs’ type parameter. This function is useful for applications that know in advance how many specific types of objects will be created.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > type :\n  > The type of object to preallocate.\n  >\n  > numObjs :\n  > The number of objects to preallocate.\n\n`purge`()\n**`purge()`**\n\nClose and delete design from memory, regardless of its reference count. All references to objects stored in this design will be invalid.\n\nIf the purged design is a Pcell supermaster, all submasters will be purged as well.\n\nRaises\nemcPurgeOnDesignBeingPurged :\n\n*static* `recover`(*libNameIn*, *cellNameIn*, *viewNameIn*, *type*)\n**`recover(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, type: emySaveRecoverType) -\\> emyDesign`**\n\nRecover the design using the specified emySaveRecoverType type of database for the specified library, cell, and view.If the recover type exists for the design, the design is opened in read-only mode and read into memory. If this function is called with the emySaveRecoverType of emcCriticalSaveType, and the critical save type file does not exist, an exception is thrown.\n\nParameters\n**libNameIn :**\nThe name of the library in which the cell is located.\n\n**cellNameIn :**\nThe name of the design to recover.\n\n**viewNameIn :**\nThe name of the cellView for the design to recover.\n\n**type :**\nThe recover file type.\n\nRaises\nemcCannotFindLibrary :\n\nemcRecoverFileDoesNotExist :\n\nemcRecoverFileAlreadyOpened :\n\nemcCannotFindDesign :\n\nemcInterfaceException :\n\n`redo`()\n**`redo()`**\n\nRestores the state of the memory image of the design database to the state before the last undo. Use pre checkpoint or post checkpoint models to store records. This call reverses changes only if Undo is enabled, undo() is called for this design, and no changes have occurred since undo() last time. The redo operation starts from the latest record and restores a transaction defined by the checkpoint and undo model.\n\n`reopen`(*\\*args*)\n**`reopen(mode: emyChar)`**\n\n**`reopen()`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`reopen(mode: emyChar)`**\n\n  > Attempts to reopen this design with the specified access mode. According to the new access mode, the design content can be reset or truncated. This function changes the open mode of this design to new *mode*.\n  >\n  > The refCount of the design is not affected.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > mode :\n  > The mode in which to open the design:\n  >\n  > - ‘r’ for read.\n  >\n  > - ‘w’ for write.\n  >\n  > - ‘a’ for append.\n  >\n  > - ‘A’ for append in override mode, which is not typically used.\n  >\n  > Note\n  >\n  > *scratch* mode is used internally to create submasters for PCells and standard via masters.\n  >\n  > The following combinations are allowed for reopen:\n  >\n  > |                          |            |          |\n  > |--------------------------|------------|----------|\n  > | Old mode                 | New mode   | Allowed? |\n  > | ‘S’                      | ‘a’ or ‘w’ | Y        |\n  > | ‘s’                      | any        | N        |\n  > | ‘s’ or ‘S’               | ‘r’        | N        |\n  > | ‘r’ or ‘a’ or ‘A’ or ‘w’ | ‘s’ or ‘S’ | N        |\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotDowngradeDatabase :\n  > The database was opened in A mode and cannot be reopened in ‘a’ mode\n  >\n  > emcCannotSetModeToScratch :\n  >\n  > emcCannotChangeFromScratchMode :\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpenInAppendMode :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmpyDatabase and cannot be opened in append mode\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnOpenInEditableMode :\n  > The design contains new features that are not understood by this version of EmpyDatabase and cannot be opened in ‘a’ppend or ‘A’ppend mode\n  >\n  > emcInvalidOperationOnDesignBeingPurged :\n  >\n  > emcCannotUpgradeOverwrittenDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcCannotLockDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcNewerDatabaseOnDisk :\n  >\n  > **See Also:**\n  >\n  > > `open`\n\n- **`reopen()`**\n\n  > Increments the refCount of the design.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcInvalidOperationOnDesignBeingPurged :\n\n`revert`()\n**`revert()`**\n\nReturns the design to the state when it was first opened. If opened in read or attach mode, reload the design content from disk. If the design is turned on in write mode, it will be truncated to contain no data. The designed access mode is not affected.\n\nIf revert is called on a design in read or attach mode, the current copy on disk is read. If this process or another process has changed it since it was originally opened, a newer version will appear after the revert call.\n\nNote\n\nAll references to objects stored in this design become invalid.\n\nThe revert call can throw any exception thrown by `open`.\n\n`save`(*\\*args*)\n**`save()`**\n\n**`save(type: emySaveRecoverType)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`save()`**\n\n  > Saves the design to disk. The design remains open, and all data in its memory (including undo data) remains valid.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcSaveReadOnlyDesign :\n  >\n  > emcSaveScratchModeDesign :\n  > Cannot save a submaster or scratch design.\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnSave :\n  > Design contains incompatible data and cannot be saved.\n  >\n  > emcCannotLockDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcNewerDatabaseOnDisk :\n\n- **`save(type: emySaveRecoverType)`**\n\n  > Saves the design to disk as the specified database recovery type. The key save and restore save types of databases are used to restore lost data. The original database file is not updated, the flag indicating whether to modify the design is not updated, and the undo stack is preserved. This save transaction will not trigger any observers. undo stack is preserved. No observers are fired by this save transaction.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > type :\n  > A value that specifies either emcAutoSaveType or emcCriticalSaveType.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcSaveReadOnlyDesign :\n  >\n  > emcSaveScratchModeDesign :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveUnboundSuperMaster :\n  >\n  > emcCannotLockDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcNewerDatabaseOnDisk :\n\n`saveAs`(*\\*args*)\n**`saveAs(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName)`**\n\n**`saveAs(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, renameTopModule: emyBoolean)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`saveAs(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName)`**\n\n  > Saves the design to disk. The design remains open, and all data in its memory (including undo data) remains valid.\n  >\n  > The application will be able to use `saveAs` to save the contents of the temporary design to disk. If the temporary design contains an instance of the temporary design, it is not allowed to use saveAs() to save the temporary design. The existing restrictions of StdVia or Pcell subMaster will not change.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The new library name.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The new cell name.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The new view name.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveAsRecursiveDesign :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveUnboundSuperMaster :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnSave :\n  >\n  > emcMismatchViewTypeForSaveAs :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveDesignWithScratchInst :\n  >\n  > emcCannotLockDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcNewerDatabaseOnDisk :\n\n- **`saveAs(libNameIn: emyScalarName, cellNameIn: emyScalarName, viewNameIn: emyScalarName, renameTopModule: emyBoolean)`**\n\n  > Saves this design to disk under the specified lib/cell/view name. The renameTopModule parameter allows the user to specify if the top module should be renamed to the new cell name.\n  >\n  > **Parameters:**\n  >\n  > libNameIn :\n  > The new library name.\n  >\n  > cellNameIn :\n  > The new cell name.\n  >\n  > viewNameIn :\n  > The new view name.\n  >\n  > renameTopModule :\n  > Specifies whether or not the top module should be renamed to be the same as the new cell name.\n  >\n  > **Raises:**\n  >\n  > emcCannotFindLibrary :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveAsRecursiveDesign :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveUnboundSuperMaster :\n  >\n  > emcDesignCannotGetWriteAccess :\n  >\n  > emcDataNotCompatibleOnSave :\n  >\n  > emcMismatchViewTypeForSaveAs :\n  >\n  > emcModuleExists :\n  >\n  > emcCannotSaveDesignWithScratchInst :\n  >\n  > emcCannotLockDatabase :\n  >\n  > emcNewerDatabaseOnDisk :\n\n`scalarize`()\n**`scalarize()`**\n\nPromotes a single bit connection to an explicit state and demotes a multi-bit connection to an implicit state. This function is applied to ensure that all single bit connections can be used to support implementation data, and restrict explicit editing of multi bit connections, which may lead to inconsistency with constituent bits.\n\nSingle-bit connectivity is represented by bit Nets (scalarNets and busNetBits), bitTerms (scalarTerms and busTermBits), bitInsts (scalarInsts and vectorInstBits), and single-bit connections.\n\n`setCellType`(*cellType*)\n**`setCellType(cellType: emyCellType)`**\n\nSets the cell type for this design.\n\nRaises\nemcInvalidMaster :\n\n`setCp`(*\\*args*)\n**`setCp()`**\n\n**`setCp(checkPointID: emyUInt4)`**\n\nHint\n\nThis is an overloaded function. See below for more details.\n\n- **`setCp()`**\n\n  > Sets the undo checkpoint for this design. Checkpoints mark the current state of this design. Every time `undo` is called, the design content will be restored to the previous state when *undo* is enabled.\n\n- **`setCp(checkPointID: emyUInt4)`**\n\n  > This function is used to set the undo checkpoint of the current design. The checkpoint marks the current status of the current design. Every time `undo` is called, the design content will be restored to the previous state when \\* undo \\* is enabled. Checkpoints have user specified IDs to help track checkpoints in this design and multiple designs.\n\n`undefineSuperMaster`()\n**`undefineSuperMaster()`**\n\nConverts the pCell super host to a regular (non-pCell) design. emyPcellDef will be discarded and parameters will be converted to emyDesign properties.\n\nAll submasters of this supermster will be destroyed, and all instances of these submasters will be bound to the former supermster (now neither submaster nor supermster). Instance parameters will be converted to properties. Other contents of the design are not affected.\n\nRaises\nemcCannotModifyReadModeDesign :\n\nemcInvalidSuperMaster :\n\n`undo`()\n**`undo()`**\n\nRestore changes made to the memory image of this design database. Undo records use pre checkpoint or post checkpoint model storage. This function returns a checkpoint to the most recent checkpoint and reverses the changes saved in the undo record. Undo starts with the most recent record and reverses a transaction defined by the checkpoint and undo model.\n\nNote\n\nOnly changes to managed objects in the emyDesign are undone. Utility objects that contain pointers to a managed object such as an `emyRouteObjectArray` are not affected.\n\n`unsetCp`()\n**`unsetCp()`**\n\nDelete the last checkpoint in the current design, and there is no undo record after the last checkpoint. Otherwise, an exception is thrown. (Refer to `getNumUndoCpRecs` to get the number of undo records after the checkpoint.)\n\nRaises\nemcUndoNotEnabled :\n\nemcCannotUnsetNonEmptyCp :\n\nTip\n\nMore properties are supported in this class, refer to here for more details.\n\n---\n\n## 补充说明（通过测试验证）\n\n# emyDesign API 测试经验总结与丰富描述\n\n## 概述\n`emyDesign` 类是 pyAether EDA 工具中的核心类，代表一个设计数据库单元。它管理设计的三个域：Block Domain（物理域）、Module Domain（逻辑域）和 Occurrence Domain（实例域）。该类提供了丰富的功能用于设计创建、打开、查询和操作。\n\n## API 功能分析\n\n### 主要功能\n1. **设计生命周期管理**：创建、打开、保存、关闭设计\n2. **设计属性查询**：获取设计的基本信息、状态、类型等\n3. **设计操作**：撤销/重做、时间戳管理、内存优化等\n4. **设计集合访问**：获取约束组、分析点、值等集合\n5. **静态方法**：设计查找、存在性检查、版本信息等\n\n### 关键方法分类\n\n#### 1. 设计状态查询\n- `isScratch()`: 检查是否为临时设计\n- `isModified()`: 检查设计是否被修改\n- `isConsistent()`: 检查设计一致性\n- `isTechBound()`: 检查是否绑定到技术库\n- `getMode()`: 获取设计打开模式（'r', 'w', 'a', 'A', 's', 'S'）\n\n#### 2. 设计类型检查\n- `isSuperMaster()`: 检查是否为 Pcell 超级主设计\n- `isSubMaster()`: 检查是否为 Pcell 子主设计\n- `isStdViaMaster()`: 检查是否为标准通孔主设计\n\n#### 3. 时间戳管理\n- `areTimeStampsEnabled()`: 检查时间戳是否启用\n- `enableTimeStamps()`: 启用/禁用时间戳\n- `getCreateTime()`: 获取创建时间\n- `getLastSavedTime()`: 获取最后保存时间\n- `getReadInTime()`: 获取读取时间\n\n#### 4. 撤销/重做功能\n- `isUndoEnabled()`: 检查撤销是否启用\n- `enableUndo()`: 启用/禁用撤销功能\n- `setCp()`: 设置检查点\n- `getCpID()`: 获取检查点ID\n- `getNumUndoRecsOnCp()`: 获取检查点上的撤销记录数\n- `getNumUndoCpRecs()`: 获取撤销检查点记录数\n- `getEffectiveUndoModel()`: 获取有效的撤销模型\n- `getUndoModel()`: 获取撤销模型\n\n#### 5. 内存管理\n- `calcVMSize()`: 计算虚拟内存使用量\n- `minimizeVM()`: 最小化虚拟内存使用\n\n#### 6. 设计集合访问\n- `getAnalysisPoints()`: 获取分析点集合\n- `getConstraintGroups()`: 获取约束组集合\n- `getValues()`: 获取值集合\n- `getConstraintGroupHeaders()`: 获取约束组头集合\n- `getConstraintParams()`: 获取约束参数集合\n\n#### 7. 静态方法\n- `getRevNumber()`: 获取内部格式版本号\n- `getOpenDesigns()`: 获取打开的设计集合\n- `exists()`: 检查设计是否存在\n- `find()`: 查找设计\n- `findScratch()`: 查找临时设计\n\n## 测试经验总结\n\n### 成功测试的方法\n在测试过程中，我们成功验证了以下方法的可用性和基本功能：\n\n1. **静态方法**：\n   - `getRevNumber()`: 成功返回内部格式版本号（整数）\n   - `getOpenDesigns()`: 成功返回打开的设计集合\n\n2. **设计状态查询**（通过已打开的设计对象）：\n   - `isScratch()`: 返回布尔值，指示是否为临时设计\n   - `getMode()`: 返回设计模式字符\n   - `getRefCount()`: 返回引用计数（非负整数）\n   - `isConsistent()`: 返回设计一致性状态\n   - `isTechBound()`: 返回技术库绑定状态\n\n3. **时间戳功能**：\n   - `areTimeStampsEnabled()`: 返回时间戳启用状态\n   - `enableTimeStamps()`: 成功启用/禁用时间戳\n\n4. **撤销功能**：\n   - `isUndoEnabled()`: 返回撤销启用状态\n   - `enableUndo()`: 成功启用撤销功能\n   - `setCp()`: 成功设置检查点\n   - `getCpID()`: 返回检查点ID\n   - `getNumUndoRecsOnCp()`: 返回检查点上的撤销记录数\n\n5. **内存管理**：\n   - `calcVMSize()`: 成功计算并返回虚拟内存使用量（字节）\n\n6. **设计集合访问**：\n   - `getAnalysisPoints()`: 返回分析点集合（可能为空）\n   - `getConstraintGroups()`: 返回约束组集合（可能为空）\n   - `getValues()`: 返回值集合（可能为空）\n\n### 参数使用技巧\n\n#### 1. 设计打开参数\n- `aeOpenDesign()` 函数接受字符串参数，但需要设计已存在\n- `emyDesign.openScratch()` 需要 `emyScalarName` 对象，创建这些对象需要正确处理命名空间\n- 视图类型必须使用有效的保留视图类型：`maskLayout`, `schematic`, `schematicSymbol`, `netlist`, 或 `hierDesign`\n\n#### 2. 错误处理\n- 当设计不存在时，`aeOpenDesign()` 返回 `False` 而不是抛出异常\n- 参数类型不匹配会导致详细的错误消息，显示可用的函数原型\n- 使用 `try-except` 块处理可能的方法调用异常\n\n#### 3. 库操作\n- `dbCreateLib()` 成功创建库并返回库对象\n- `dbOpenLib()` 成功打开现有库\n\n### 常见错误和解决方法\n\n#### 1. 设计打开失败\n**问题**：`aeOpenDesign()` 返回 `False`\n**原因**：设计不存在或权限不足\n**解决**：确保设计存在，或使用写模式创建新设计\n\n#### 2. 参数类型错误\n**问题**：`Wrong number or type of arguments for overloaded function`\n**原因**：参数类型不匹配，如传递字符串而不是 `emyScalarName`\n**解决**：查看错误消息中的函数原型，使用正确的参数类型\n\n#### 3. 抽象类实例化\n**问题**：`No constructor defined - class is abstract`\n**原因**：尝试实例化抽象类如 `emyNameSpace`\n**解决**：查找正确的工厂方法或使用默认构造函数\n\n#### 4. 视图类型无效\n**问题**：`Invalid reservedViewType name`\n**原因**：使用了无效的视图类型名称\n**解决**：使用有效的保留视图类型常量\n\n### 返回值分析\n\n#### 布尔返回值\n- `isScratch()`, `isModified()`, `isConsistent()` 等返回 `True`/`False`\n- 表示设计状态的布尔属性\n\n#### 整数返回值\n- `getRefCount()`: 返回非负整数，表示设计引用计数\n- `calcVMSize()`: 返回整数，表示虚拟内存使用量（字节）\n- `getRevNumber()`: 返回整数，表示内部格式版本号\n\n#### 集合返回值\n- `getAnalysisPoints()`, `getConstraintGroups()` 等返回集合对象\n- 可以使用 `len()` 获取元素数量\n- 空集合表示设计中没有相应元素\n\n#### 枚举/字符返回值\n- `getMode()`: 返回字符表示设计模式\n- `getUndoModel()`: 返回撤销模型枚举值\n\n## 最佳实践\n\n### 1. 设计打开流程\n```python\nimport pyAether as pa\n\n# 初始化环境\npa.emyInitAether(\"-adv\")\n\n# 打开库\nlib = pa.dbOpenLib(\"TECHLIB\", \"r\")\n\n# 尝试打开设计\nsuccess = pa.aeOpenDesign(lib=\"TECHLIB\", cell=\"cell_name\", view=\"layout\", mode=\"r\")\nif success:\n    design = pa.aeGetCurDesign()\n    # 使用设计对象\n    # ...\n    design.close()\n```\n\n### 2. 错误处理模式\n```python\ntry:\n    # 尝试操作\n    result = design.someMethod()\nexcept Exception as e:\n    # 处理特定错误\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n    # 回退或重试\n```\n\n### 3. 资源管理\n```python\n# 确保设计被正确关闭\ndesign = None\ntry:\n    design = pa.aeGetCurDesign()\n    # 使用设计\n    # ...\nfinally:\n    if design:\n        design.close()\n```\n\n### 4. 状态检查\n```python\n# 在执行操作前检查设计状态\nif design.isModified():\n    print(\"Design has unsaved changes\")\n    \nif design.isScratch():\n    print(\"This is a scratch design (cannot be saved directly)\")\n    \nif not design.isConsistent():\n    print(\"Design is inconsistent\")\n```\n\n## 注意事项\n\n### 1. 设计模式\n- 设计可以处于不同模式：'r'（读）、'w'（写）、'a'（追加）、'A'（追加覆盖）、's' 或 'S'（临时）\n- 临时设计（scratch）不能直接保存，需要使用 `saveAs()`\n\n### 2. 引用计数\n- 每个 `open()` 调用增加引用计数\n- 每个 `close()` 调用减少引用计数\n- 引用计数为0时，设计从内存中清除\n\n### 3. 撤销系统\n- 撤销功能需要显式启用：`enableUndo(True)`\n- 检查点标记设计状态：`setCp()`\n- 撤销模型影响撤销行为：`getEffectiveUndoModel()`\n\n### 4. 时间戳\n- 时间戳默认启用\n- 可以禁用以提高性能：`enableTimeStamps(False)`\n- 时间信息包括创建时间、最后保存时间和读取时间\n\n### 5. 内存管理\n- 大型设计可能消耗大量内存\n- 使用 `calcVMSize()` 监控内存使用\n- 使用 `minimizeVM()` 优化内存（性能影响）\n\n## 测试代码执行情况\n\n### 成功执行的测试\n1. **静态方法测试**：`getRevNumber()`, `getOpenDesigns()`\n2. **设计状态查询**：所有状态检查方法\n3. **时间戳管理**：启用/禁用和查询\n4. **撤销功能**：启用、检查点设置、状态查询\n5. **内存管理**：虚拟内存计算\n6. **设计集合访问**：获取各种集合\n\n### 遇到的挑战\n1. **设计创建/打开**：无法成功创建或打开设计进行完整测试\n2. **参数类型**：`emyScalarName` 和 `emyNameSpace` 对象创建困难\n3. **库设计访问**：TECHLIB 中的设计无法用测试的视图名称打开\n\n### 建议的改进\n1. 提供更详细的错误信息帮助调试设计打开问题\n2. 简化 `emyScalarName` 和 `emyNameSpace` 的创建过程\n3. 提供设计列表和视图枚举功能\n4. 增强示例代码展示完整工作流程\n\n## 结论\n\n`emyDesign` 类提供了完整的设计管理功能，但需要正确处理参数类型和设计状态。测试表明核心功能正常工作，但设计创建和打开流程需要进一步探索。建议在实际使用前充分测试设计打开流程，并正确处理错误情况。\n\nAPI 文档详细但需要实际示例来展示完整用法。对于生产使用，建议：\n1. 建立设计打开/创建的可靠流程\n2. 实现完整的错误处理\n3. 监控设计状态和内存使用\n4. 合理使用撤销和时间戳功能\n\n---\n## Properties (emyDesign)\n\n| Property | Description | Writable |\n|----------|-------------|----------|\n| `all` | The string which includes all properties of the emyDesign | unknown |\n| `design` | The emyDesign class which represent the design | unknown |\n| `prop` | The tuple which includes prop of the design | unknown |\n| `bbox` | The emyBoxF class which represent the user unit box of the design | unknown |\n| `dbuBox` | The emyBox class which represent the database unit box of the design | unknown |\n| `lib` | The emyLib class wh